course: history
lesson: 71
title: What trade carried: tomatoes, tea, horses, words, religions and diseases
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Open the kitchen cupboard and you open a map of the world: the tomatoes in the pasta sauce came from the Americas, the tea from China, the coffee from Ethiopia and Yemen, the sugar from New Guinea and India, and the chocolate from Mexico.
explain: After 1492 ships began to link the Americas with the rest of the world, and plants, animals, people and germs crossed the ocean both ways; historians call this the Columbian Exchange.
explain: In Europa Universalis a province simply «produces sugar» or «produces tea» from 1444, as if it always had; in real life many of those crops had only just arrived, and sugar in the Caribbean was grown by enslaved Africans, so a cheap treat had a terrible price.
explain: Trade carried invisible things too: words like «tea» and «chai», religions like Islam to Indonesia, and diseases like smallpox, which killed far more people in the Americas than any army did.
explain: So when someone says a food is «always» from somewhere, ask: since when, and who brought it?
fact: Tomatoes come from the Americas; they reached Europe in the 1500s, Italians first grew them as curiosities, and tomato sauce became common in Italy only in the 1700s and 1800s.
fact: Potatoes were first grown in the Andes of South America thousands of years ago, reached Europe in the 1500s and spread across Europe, Ukraine included, in the 1700s and 1800s; when potato disease struck Ireland in 1845–1852, about a million people died and about a million left.
fact: Chilli peppers come from the Americas and reached Asia in the 1500s with Portuguese and other traders; red, spicy kimchi became common in Korea in the 1700s.
fact: Sugar cane was first grown in New Guinea, and people in India first made crystal sugar; from the 1500s Europeans set up sugar plantations in Brazil and the Caribbean worked by enslaved Africans.
fact: Tea comes from China, where it was drunk for thousands of years; it reached Britain in the 1600s, and from the 1830s British companies grew tea in India.
fact: Coffee drinking began in Ethiopia and Yemen and spread through the Muslim world; coffee houses opened in Istanbul in the 1500s and in London in 1652.
fact: People in Mexico and Central America drank chocolate, often bitter and spicy, for thousands of years; the Aztecs used cacao beans as money, and an English firm sold one of the first chocolate bars in 1847.
fact: Horses died out in the Americas about 10,000 years ago and came back with the Spanish in the 1500s; peoples like the Comanche and Lakota became great horse riders, and a 2023 study with Indigenous scholars suggested horses spread on the Plains earlier than European records say.
fact: After 1492 smallpox, measles and other diseases reached the Americas, where people had no protection; in some places the population fell by up to nine-tenths, though the exact numbers are debated.
fact: Most languages call tea «te» or «cha»: words like «tea» came by sea from the coast of China, where people said «te», and words like «chai» and «çay» came by land routes from places that said «cha».
fact: Islam spread to Indonesia mostly through traders and teachers from the 1200s onwards, and Indonesia now has more Muslims than any other country; Buddhism also travelled from India to China and Japan along trade routes.
exercise: Where did tomatoes first come from? | The Americas | Italy | China | Ancient Greece
exercise: When did tomato sauce become common in Italy? | In the 1700s and 1800s | Back in Roman times | Before about 500 CE | Only in the 1990s, with pizza shops
exercise: Where were potatoes first grown? | In the Andes of South America | On the black-earth plains of Ukraine | In the wet fields of Ireland | In ancient Egypt
exercise: Why was depending on potatoes dangerous for Ireland in the 1840s? | A potato disease ruined the crop people relied on | Potatoes were banned by a new law passed in London | Potatoes cannot grow at all in rainy weather | Everyone was tired of eating potatoes every day
exercise: Where do chilli peppers come from? | The Americas | Korea, long ago | Southern India | Thailand
exercise: When did red, spicy kimchi become common in Korea? | In the 1700s | 5,000 years ago | In the 1950s | It never did
exercise: Where was sugar cane first grown? | New Guinea | The Caribbean | England | Brazil
exercise: Who worked the sugar plantations of the Caribbean and Brazil? | Enslaved Africans | Paid European farmers | Robots and machines | Monks from Ireland
exercise: Where does tea come from? | China | Britain | Brazil | Mexico
exercise: Where did coffee drinking begin? | Ethiopia and Yemen | The USA, in Seattle | France, in Paris cafés | Japan, in old Kyoto
exercise: What did the Aztecs use cacao beans for, besides drinks? | As money | As building bricks | As medicine for horses | As writing ink
exercise: When did horses come back to the Americas? | With the Spanish in the 1500s | With the Vikings in 1000 | They never left | With the first farmers 10,000 years ago
exercise: Why did diseases like smallpox kill so many people in the Americas? | People there had no protection against them | Americans never washed | The diseases were spread on purpose by every ship | Only soldiers got sick
exercise: Why do some languages say «tea» and others «chai»? | The words travelled by sea or by land from places that said «te» or «cha» | Each country made up its own word at random when tea first arrived there | «Chai» is just a spelling mistake that people copied for centuries | The Queen of England chose both words for her tea merchants
exercise: How did Islam mostly reach Indonesia? | Through traders and teachers | Through a single great conquest | Through radio and television | It began there first
exercise: Which country has the most Muslims today? | Indonesia | Saudi Arabia | Egypt | Turkey
test: Luca leans over the fence: "Nah nonna, Italians always had tomatoes, that's just facts." ("Nonna" means grandma in Italian.) What does nonna know? | Tomatoes came from the Americas and reached Italy only in the 1500s | Luca is right: the Romans already ate tomato pizza every day | Tomatoes came to Italy from China, brought back by Marco Polo in 1295 | Tomatoes were invented in a laboratory in Naples in 1950
test: Oksana goes, "Borshch has potatoes, so potatoes are proper Ukrainian, aren't they?" What's the right answer? | Potatoes came from the Andes and spread to Ukraine in the 1700s and 1800s | Yes, potatoes grew wild only in Ukraine and spread from there to the rest of the world | Potatoes came to Ukraine from Ireland back in Roman times | Potatoes were brought to Ukraine by the Vikings in about 900
test: Ji-woo's friend: "Korean food's been spicy since forever, no cap." ("No cap" means no lie.) Ji-woo's halmeoni (grandma) shakes her head. Why? | Chillies came from the Americas and red kimchi became common only in the 1700s | Koreans never ate chilli at all, not even today | Kimchi was only invented in 2005 for a cooking show on Korean television | Chillies are a Korean plant that was later taken to Mexico by Korean sailors
test: Callum's Yorkshire gran: "Nowt more British than a cuppa, love." ("Nowt" means nothing.) What's the gentle correction? | Tea is Chinese; Britain only got it in the 1600s | Gran is right, love: tea grows wild all over Yorkshire | Tea came to Britain from Brazil along with coffee | Tea was invented in a London shop in 1900 by a grocer
test: Abdi's mate: "Coffee's from you? Fr? I thought Starbucks." ("Fr" means for real.) What is true? | Coffee drinking began in Ethiopia and Yemen centuries before any American café | Your mate's right, fr: coffee was invented in Seattle by the first Starbucks | Coffee came from Italy only, where espresso was born and later spread | Coffee is really a kind of chocolate from Mexico
test: Lucía's mate: "Money you can eat? Bussin'." ("Bussin'" means really tasty.) What did the Aztecs really do with cacao? | Used beans as money and drank chocolate, often bitter and spicy | Ate sweet milk chocolate bars every single day, like we do now | Burned the beans as fuel to keep their houses and temples warm at night | Fed the beans only to their horses and cattle
test: In a western film, Plains peoples gallop on horses. Ben's cousin says, "They had horses since the Ice Age, deffo." What's more exact? | Horses died out there and came back with the Spanish, then spread fast | Yes, the very same horses lived there for ever and were never gone | Horses were never ridden by Plains peoples; the films made it up | Horses came to America with the Vikings in about the year 1000, on longships
test: Jack's teacher says, "Sugar has a sad history." Jack: "How can sweets be sad, innit?" What does she mean? | Sugar was grown on plantations worked by enslaved Africans | Sugar makes people cry when they eat too much of it | Sugar was always free for everyone, so farmers were poor | Sugar was only grown by kings, who kept it all for themselves
test: In Europa Universalis, Mateo's Spain conquers the Aztecs with a few clicks and a big army. What does the game leave out? | Diseases like smallpox killed huge numbers before and during the fighting | Spain really had millions of soldiers there, many more than the Aztecs did | The Aztecs had no army at all and never fought back | Nothing, the game shows exactly what happened
test: Ava says "tea", her Turkish friend says "çay", her Portuguese friend says "chá". What story do the words tell? | They show which routes tea travelled on, by sea or by land | They are random sounds with no history behind them at all | All three were made up by one king who loved tea | Tea was first grown in Turkey and its name spread west from there
test: Aisha: "Indonesia's got the most Muslims? But it's nowhere near Arabia, wallahi." ("Wallahi" means I swear.) Why is it true? | Traders and teachers carried Islam across the sea from the 1200s | Arabian armies sailed over and conquered all of Indonesia by force | Indonesia was once a part of Arabia, joined by land | It isn't true, wallahi: Saudi Arabia has the most Muslims
test: What would have happened if ships had never crossed the Atlantic after 1492? | Europe, Asia and Africa would have no tomatoes, potatoes, chillies or cacao | Nothing would change in any kitchen | Italy would have more tomatoes | Tea would never have reached Britain
exam: Identify the region in which the potato was first domesticated. | The Andes of South America | The plains of Ukraine | The island of Ireland | The Nile valley of ancient Egypt
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the labour employed upon the sugar plantations of the Caribbean from the sixteenth century? | It was performed principally by enslaved Africans | It was performed principally by salaried European farmers | It was performed principally by machinery | It was performed principally by monastic orders
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dissemination of the word for tea. | Forms resembling «tea» travelled by sea and forms resembling «chai» travelled by land | All forms derive from a decree of the English Crown | The word was invented independently in each language | All forms derive from the Turkish language
exam: Ascertain the principal means whereby Islam was conveyed to the Indonesian archipelago. | Through merchants and teachers from the thirteenth century onwards | Through a single military conquest by Arabian armies | Through radio broadcasts in the twentieth century | Through the Spanish voyages of the sixteenth century
